// The loop's own induction variable is a real recurrence -- `i = i * 3 + 1`
// reads the previous value of `i` -- and it is deliberately not counted as one
// of the independent chains. The condition is tested before every copy, so `i`
// sits between one copy and the next: no copy begins until the test and the
// update have resolved, which means there is no overlap there to find.
//
// Counting it would make this loop look like two independent chains and unroll
// it. `s` is the only chain, so it stays rolled. This probe is the one that
// fails if the exclusion is dropped.
int main() {
    var s = 0;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < 1000000) {
        s = (s * 31 + i) % 1000000007;
        i = i * 3 + 1;
    }
    print(s);
    return 0;
}
